#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175659 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/id #uuid "ca9ba6cf-860f-5868-b91f-1dff84406f22" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14357" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y = 4x - 1$ and passes through the point $(-8,7)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $l_1$ denote the line $y = 4x - 1$, and let $l_2$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of $l_2$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of $l_1$. Since $l_1$ has a slope of $4$, the line $l_2$ must have a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nand pass through the point\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1)=(-8,7)\n$$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y - y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\[5pt] y - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-(-8)) \\\\[5pt] y - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x+8) \\\\[5pt] y - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x - 2 \\\\[5pt] y &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x + 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175661 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "ca9ec8da-5f74-53dc-afa7-e6cc965fcc95" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14363" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope $m_1$ of the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1} &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 1}{-2 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{-2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 4 &= \\frac{2}{3}(x - 3) \\\\\ny - 4 &= \\frac{2}{3}x - 2 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175665 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "49cee7e9-29a5-5e1e-bc13-7dbabaaea405" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14730" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,2)$ and is perpendicular to $3x-2y=1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $3x-2y=1$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 2y &= 1 \\\\\n- 2y &= -3x + 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{3}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe given line has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nso our perpendicular line must have a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nand pass through the point\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (6,2)\n$$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 2 &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x - 6) \\\\\ny - 2 &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + 4 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d61639-c435-562a-97a3-b12c12a64b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246803} {:answer/id #uuid "fd636abb-fd4a-5e3b-be7b-2a1101756255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246804} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae18454-d341-5cc7-a2fd-d2f7a037e52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246805} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1e04fa-e872-5646-ad5d-17579f4684f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246806} {:answer/id #uuid "850e5bba-4731-5599-b16e-7a8c7a7e037d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "850e5bba-4731-5599-b16e-7a8c7a7e037d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246807} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e38ef1a-4ee3-531d-9211-cd1bb4399c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246802}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a074bb7c-f733-505c-a777-082de91b8ef3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103449" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1980acbe-516b-5de3-9b3a-9f748634a857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{4}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246810} {:answer/id #uuid "072f2194-5ab6-5087-a400-4b663f6fdd1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246811} {:answer/id #uuid "aa17e738-55de-5a4b-9fce-3a293aa00ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246812} {:answer/id #uuid "8a75a8ca-4c3a-5af5-8fd9-918b1d690275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246813} {:answer/id #uuid "88a7fdca-71da-547f-8e38-73ad5f0a19b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa17e738-55de-5a4b-9fce-3a293aa00ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246812} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3747c76-2265-5b79-a09e-d2ce5fc7e463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246809}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be690892-d1ee-5b6d-ab92-cca99b162dcd" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103455" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(8,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(8,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{5}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{5}{4}x-10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{4}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0735bfe-94a4-58e2-a10b-385d490997ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0735bfe-94a4-58e2-a10b-385d490997ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246817} :answer-field/id #uuid "97e1f251-70da-5c6a-8984-d48773fc9069" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246816}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63ce0ff0-d49c-5492-b89a-ea6a5e332526" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-215768" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-4 & =x \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b322489e-e53b-5226-af85-c6d93ba9eb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246820} {:answer/id #uuid "58c1935e-ff78-5933-b4e4-51a93992c003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246821} {:answer/id #uuid "18ba6f8c-015d-54f0-9a37-e5112a5a46b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246822} {:answer/id #uuid "deca52ed-b51e-5fd8-b96b-ecd8190eb94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246823} {:answer/id #uuid "703bd43f-1439-51e0-8b47-3575c00f6846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703bd43f-1439-51e0-8b47-3575c00f6846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246824} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8569deb-6cf7-5d5c-84ef-2474c64086d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebba1590-9e65-500b-8c4b-a4d7ba7a650f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-23631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc55c0b3-1870-5d3e-acd7-aff99013c4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc55c0b3-1870-5d3e-acd7-aff99013c4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246827} :answer-field/id #uuid "c46413f9-b558-53bd-9f22-11d016cba92f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246826}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "130d5595-7cbe-51db-aac8-83150d0860ae" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-237031" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to $y=4x-6$ that passes through the point $(8,-3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=4x-6,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(8,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1299bd78-18f8-50fb-8baf-2c768dace883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246830} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc16bf-8e6b-5824-a7d5-7a0dec6c3cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1299bd78-18f8-50fb-8baf-2c768dace883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246830} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a66b41c-db0d-5cca-912a-80b5be4da9fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d7b950d-4468-5f50-9499-9d01afa18ed5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-237193" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/5add9f877dc2e93b1c8e562794aa64c83d180f5c683735055ee24f8a190e8740.png)\n\nThe equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=-4x-3$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$  What is the equation of ${l}_{2}?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $-4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-4) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1813979a-422c-5365-beae-dc322fe363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1813979a-422c-5365-beae-dc322fe363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246834} :answer-field/id #uuid "0827810c-2459-54cc-bf9f-a6afaf30eccd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246833}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e4defe2-68c2-5032-be33-7cb859509d28" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-237197" :question/problem "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. If the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(6,-1),$ what is the value of $b?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{2}{5},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(6,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-6) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+15 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57488bd3-83d5-5db6-94fd-b8a126d276a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57488bd3-83d5-5db6-94fd-b8a126d276a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246837} :answer-field/id #uuid "23819259-60f2-55cc-98b9-8bbc4ba2b535" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246836}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3e3189e-08d0-518b-8225-73f15324974f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-237224" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6}{5}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6}{5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a1e4813-3faa-51f3-a6c7-5f380a270cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a1e4813-3faa-51f3-a6c7-5f380a270cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246840} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f1759f-0b67-5953-a229-7ecd8422ffd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62a37dcc-7584-5c66-9685-db6471626299" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-237230" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,3)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $5x+y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-5x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-5x+2.$ The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+4) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdc38579-e69e-53f0-b1d2-d53f0ff0043c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdc38579-e69e-53f0-b1d2-d53f0ff0043c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246843} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6bd0d56-e288-5c4a-9f2c-1d0c00ef8147" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246842}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f783ab8b-742a-5e04-8610-0016de741818" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-237231" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,0)$ and is perpendicular to $x-6y=-3?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $x-6y=-3$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6y & =-3 \\\\ -6y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-6.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-6$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-6(x-(-7)) \\\\ y & =-6(x+7) \\\\ y & =-6x-42\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81266fc1-fe0c-51e1-9374-4ba7b1deca75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246846} {:answer/id #uuid "a920e7b8-ecbd-5444-a4b7-a9bf8fdfc6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246847} {:answer/id #uuid "605d03d7-51e9-53a3-887a-8482ecc9319e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246848} {:answer/id #uuid "097d4e64-f405-57b6-bd5b-957c47f6e9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y=19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246849} {:answer/id #uuid "ed224373-3e75-571a-8299-ea9867c56239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a920e7b8-ecbd-5444-a4b7-a9bf8fdfc6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246847} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4e7f27-bcce-5fe9-a537-b648f9f75292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c19ac983-6286-56e7-bf4f-85d61c5577fb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-252524" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes, which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3) \\\\ 2(y-4) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-x+3 \\\\ 2y & =-x+11 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x+2y & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46fa97f-4ae5-5696-9899-6c8f09cdf115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246853} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a49f2-0382-59c8-a332-fd0e435b7bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246854} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f924c-0567-5497-be06-5d4ff9eeb0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246855} {:answer/id #uuid "773f0b0b-9b58-5637-ab2e-a4f69d3b9435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246856} {:answer/id #uuid "826b35cd-a0a2-5e29-bfc3-52e7878fb547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826b35cd-a0a2-5e29-bfc3-52e7878fb547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246857} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9da41e2-2923-5cf4-91a0-ca53e7a6f47f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a15bc33a-bff2-50cc-9ade-d155c462a5f8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-44337" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1a298b8-01e3-52e6-a1eb-3bcc573a85fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246860} {:answer/id #uuid "c63c6dfc-36e4-5c7b-ac97-e9492879f707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246861} {:answer/id #uuid "553b9bed-025c-5ece-9d2d-ebc6843480b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246862} {:answer/id #uuid "3b875168-aac2-5c76-b7c3-e98d96a73bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246863} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4f76b7-128b-5c43-a55f-a6482a9af0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c63c6dfc-36e4-5c7b-ac97-e9492879f707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246861} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ead9df-d3a4-5fb9-a565-bbf300cf97b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8dccbd68-1234-5f4e-b21e-948f34cd05c0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-44606" :question/problem "The lines $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. Given that the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(-1,5),$ what is the value of $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-2$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x+1) \\\\ y-5 & =-2x-2 \\\\ y & =-2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bec70c9-5e65-5511-8369-e98b7f037611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{6}x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246867} {:answer/id #uuid "1e065ea5-b657-55dd-bf38-765441fce976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246868} {:answer/id #uuid "f4046e3e-1549-522e-95c9-1df51a0eb033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246869} {:answer/id #uuid "03483045-a4e7-57d8-a807-f45aeba59056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246870} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aa901a-66ed-510b-9799-74d42822b0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4046e3e-1549-522e-95c9-1df51a0eb033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246869} :answer-field/id #uuid "c506f7ea-f204-5fe9-8d02-39049270a1cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246866}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "894ed8a4-9322-584c-9def-aed67d0a3672" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-72567" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-6,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,6)$ and $(0,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,6)$ and $(0,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-6}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{2} \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-6)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-6,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-(-6)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+6) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc17b0d0-2ec2-56d0-a474-b79cf089d19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246874} {:answer/id #uuid "a57da595-713d-571f-943c-f851b7f0409d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246875} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d7cf86-2019-53c3-bb72-d60df9b3a383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246876} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb6ad37-ccca-55e6-a371-9cffe47a451f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246877} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f80421-001d-5610-9ddd-eaa7f85df628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d7cf86-2019-53c3-bb72-d60df9b3a383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246876} :answer-field/id #uuid "5948b3b0-f84e-5728-bd08-69af5f4152e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5b78a83-fe68-5017-a927-40809114619c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-72568" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
